{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794750971,"nonce":"i8qkXJEGGNbvFCGz4AJhdmNkY94=","message":"First nAIR program on the real engines (6b6f3337).\n\n`nair2hl program.tast out.hl Main.main` lowers an entry function and everything it calls, assembles a module (types, constant pools, natives, functions serialized from AIR to opcodes), and writes a .hl file. Calls of @:hlNative functions become native imports, with the library and symbol read from the metadata (field-level, or class-level with the prefix and snake-cased name, as genhl does).\n\nCheck: a fixture whose main is `Sys.exit(fib(32) \u0026 255)` is compiled by genhl and through nAIR. genhl's file is 40 KB because it carries the std start-up; nAIR's is 155 bytes (two functions, one native). Both return 5 on `--mode interp` and `--mode jit`; with fib(38) both return 41 on jit and hybrid. The test is crates/ash_nair_hl/tests/run.rs and needs ASH_HAXE_NATIVE and an ash binary (ASH_BIN, or target/release/ash).\n\nWhat this is and is not: a bridge. The engines take opcodes, so the facts nAIR knows beyond the bytecode do not survive it; what ran is nAIR's lowering and nothing yet of what the TAST adds. It exists to run and time the lowering on the real tiers and to compare with genhl's file.\n\nTimings, so nobody quotes them: on this Mac, fib(38), per jit tier, nAIR's file was about 2x slower on Cranelift (0.32 s against 0.16 s) and about 2x faster on LLVM (0.15 s against 0.30 s). The same genhl file measured 0.33 s, 0.16 s and 0.04 s on three consecutive runs, so the runtime's caches decide these short runs. The two files differ in shape (genhl's carries the std start-up; the opcode sequences differ), and each tier's inlining and recursion handling responds to shape. No conclusion until the NUC, longer programs and cache control.\n\nNext: grow what lowers so real benchmarks fit (objects and fields, arrays, strings), then measure properly on the NUC; and take AIR directly instead of opcodes, which is where the TAST facts start to count.","files":null}]}